    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=c554f5cb-2995-4389-aed8-64d3a14e6175": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant - Student Success Center",
      "description": "Graduate Assistant - Student Success Center | Job Opportunities British English CA English Czech Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Hungarian Italian Japanese Korean Polish Português do Brasil Romanian Swedish Thai U.S. English Vietnamese 简体中文 繁體中文 Find Opportunities U.S. English Sign In Accessibility Note If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following our Accessibility Accomodation for Applicants page. Visit page Dismiss Graduate Assistant - Student Success Center Share Post on X Apply now Job Details Posting Details Posted on September 17, 2026 Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations On-site Staff Part-Time Requisition #: GRADU001843 Description DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: Office of Student Success Center (SSC) POSITION : Temporary, Non- Exempt (9 months), Part-time (20 Hours) (2) SALARY RANGE: $16.90-$16.90 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. INTRODUCTORY PERIOD: Six (6) Months AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 17 , 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: The Student Success Center (SSC) Graduate Assistants support the University's efforts to promote student access, academic success, persistence, and belonging. Graduate Assistants work collaboratively with professional staff across the Student Success Center and may support programs and services in Disability & Accessibility Services (DAS), Tutoring & Academic Support, and other SSC initiatives. While each Graduate Assistant may have a primary area of assignment (for example, Tutoring Services or Disability and Accessibility Services) based on departmental needs, experience, and interests, all Graduate Assistants are members of the broader SSC team and may provide support across functional areas. Graduate Assistants report to the Associate Dean but work closely with and support the Director of Disability and Accessibility Services and the Assistant Director of Tutoring Services and the Writing Center.  This structure provides Graduate Assistants with experience in student support and higher education administration while allowing the SSC to respond flexibly to changing student and program needs. Duties and Responsibilities: Duties and responsibilities include but are not limited to: Student Success Center Support Assist with the development and implementation of programs designed to promote student success, persistence, self-advocacy, help-seeking, and effective academic habits. Provide support for SSC workshops, events, outreach campaigns, and student-engagement initiatives. Assist students with navigating SSC resources and connecting with appropriate University services. Develop student-facing resources, presentations, communications, and educational materials. Provide support for special projects and emerging Student Success Center initiatives. Collaborate with professional staff, undergraduate student employees, peer leaders, tutors, and other members of the SSC team. Provide front-desk or general SSC coverage as needed. Tutoring & Academic Support Depending upon assignment, responsibilities may include: Assist professional staff with the coordination and administration of subject tutoring, writing support, peer academic support, and related programs. Help coordinate group tutoring, study halls, academic workshops, embedded academic support, and other targeted academic-support initiatives. Support tutor recruitment, onboarding, training, scheduling, communication, and ongoing professional development. Assist with the development of tutor training materials, workshops, academic-success resources, and other programming. Assist with outreach designed to increase student awareness and utilization of tutoring and academic-support resources. Support programming during high-demand academic periods, including midterms and final examinations. Provide support and guidance to undergraduate peer academic-support staff within the parameters established by professional SSC staff. Disability & Accessibility Services Depending upon assignment, responsibilities may include: Assist DAS professional staff with the coordination and implementation of approved academic accommodations and accessibility services. Support administrative processes and logistics associated with accommodations and other DAS services. Assist with student communication, outreach, appointment preparation, follow-up, and resource development as directed by professional DAS staff. Support accessibility-related educational programming, workshops, awareness initiatives, and campus events. Assist with the development and maintenance of student and faculty resources related to accessibility and accommodation processes. Support data entry, records management, utilization tracking, and program assessment consistent with University policies and confidentiality requirements. Support special projects designed to improve accessibility, student self-advocacy, and disability inclusion across the University. If assigned, review accommodations paperwork and documentation and participate in student meetings with professional staff supervision. Additional Responsibilities Attend regular meetings, training, and supervision with assigned SSC professional staff. Maintain appropriate professional boundaries and safeguard confidential student information in accordance with FERPA, University policy, and applicable requirements. Exercise discretion and sound judgment when working with sensitive student information and refer matters requiring professional decision-making to appropriate staff. Maintain accurate records and complete assigned administrative responsibilities in a timely manner. Work collaboratively with campus partners and represent the Student Success Center professionally. Support divisional and/or institutional initiatives or programs. Perform other related duties consistent with the purpose and scope of the Graduate Assistant position. Schedule Expectations Graduate Assistants will generally work 15–20 hours per week, not to exceed 20 hours per week, with schedules established in consultation with their supervisor and based on academic schedules and departmental needs.  Because some student-success and academic-support activities occur outside traditional business hours, Graduate Assistants should expect some regularly scheduled or periodic evening and after-hours work. Evening responsibilities may include support for tutoring and academic-support programming, study halls, student events, testing support, and other SSC initiatives. Some daytime availability during regular University business hours will also be required. Qualifications Guidelines: Any combination of experience/training/education equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Current enrollment and good standing in an eligible University graduate program. Strong interpersonal, communication, organizational, and time-management skills. Ability to manage multiple responsibilities and work both independently and collaboratively. Strong attention to detail and ability to follow established procedures. Ability to exercise discretion and maintain confidentiality when working with student information. Interest in student success, academic support, accessibility, higher education, education, counseling, student affairs, or a related area. Availability to work a combination of daytime and evening hours based on assigned responsibilities. Preferred Qualifications Previous experience in student services, student affairs, higher education, or another student-support setting. Experience developing or facilitating workshops, trainings, educational programs, or student activities. Familiarity with academic-success strategies, executive functioning, learning strategies, or peer academic support. Experience working with disabled and/or neurodivergent students or knowledge of accessibility and inclusive educational practices. Experience working with confidential or sensitive information. Learning Outcomes and Professional Development The Graduate Assistant position is designed to provide meaningful professional experience in student success and higher education administration. Graduate Assistants will have opportunities to develop skills in program development, student support, accessibility, academic-support services, assessment, communication, leadership, and cross-functional collaboration. Because Graduate Assistants work across the Student Success Center, they will also gain exposure to how multiple student-support functions work together to address barriers, promote student development, and improve student persistence and academic success. Graduate Assistants with a primary focus in this area have the opportunity to learn about accommodation procedures in higher education and to put their coursework to practice in reviewing and understanding documentation, meeting with students, etc.  Graduate Assistants may assist with accommodation-related processes but do not independently determine student eligibility for disability accommodations, approve or deny accommodations, or make other decisions reserved for authorized DAS professional staff. Graduate Assistants have the opportunity to expand on their academic learning as related to learning theories, academic support methodologies, and working with diverse learners in higher education.  Graduate Assistants with a primary focus on Tutoring and the Writing Center may assist with mentoring and professional development of the student tutors under the supervision of the Assistant Director, but do not act as supervisors of student workers or independently assume responsibility for training. Knowledge and Skills: Graduate Assistants are anticipated to achieve the following learning outcomes from this role: Apply graduate-level coursework to professional practice in student success, higher education administration, accessibility, learning theory, and academic support. Develop knowledge of disability and accessibility services in higher education , including accommodation processes, documentation review, the interactive process, and appropriate professional boundaries. Apply learning theories and academic-support methodologies when working with diverse learners and supporting tutoring, writing, and other academic-success programs. Develop skills in program development, communication, and peer mentoring through participation in SSC programs and initiatives. Understand cross-functional approaches to student success and how advising, accessibility, tutoring, writing support, and other services work collaboratively to address barriers, promote student development, and improve persistence and academic success. Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Standard office and field setting. Duties performed are in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings, and outside at sports recreation facilities. The position requires working some evenings and weekends. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly sit for long periods; stand, and walk on a regular basis; occasionally may run; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; stoop or kneel or crouch to file; speak clearly and distinctly to answer telephones and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 20 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. BACKGROUND CHECK: Employment is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Offers are conditional and may be rescinded if disqualifying information is found or if information was withheld or falsified. Current employees may be affected if the check is not satisfactorily completed. DRIVER’S LICENSE: Certain positions require a valid driver’s license and enrollment in the DMV Government Employer Pull Notice Program to verify license status and driving record. PHYSICAL/PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: Select positions (e.g., Public Safety, Facilities, Student Affairs, Athletics, ITS) require a pre-employment physical exam, including the ability to lift 50+ lbs. Public Safety Officers must also pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. \nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
    },
    "https://recruiting2.ultipro.com/UNI1089UOR/JobBoard/6af23b67-9dd3-4a6a-bda1-676b92a4fcb4/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=fe1a41cc-e1d8-4b6e-9b68-131ee35d9e9d": {
      "title": "General Accountant",
      "description": "General Accountant | Job Opportunities British English CA English Czech Danish Deutsch Dutch Español FR French Français Canadien Hungarian Italian Japanese Korean Polish Português do Brasil Romanian Swedish Thai U.S. English Vietnamese 简体中文 繁體中文 Find Opportunities U.S. English Sign In Accessibility Note If you are a job seeker with a disability and require a reasonable accommodation to apply for one of our jobs, you will find the contact information to request the appropriate accommodation by visiting the following our Accessibility Accomodation for Applicants page. Visit page Dismiss General Accountant Share Post on X Apply now Job Details Posting Details Posted on September 16, 2026 Locations Showing 1 location California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA California - Redlands University of Redlands 1200 E Colton Ave Redlands, CA 92374, USA +0 more locations less locations On-site Admin Full-Time Requisition #: GENER001841 Description Position Control: 8052 DEPARTMENT/ADMINISTRATION: Office of Business and Finance POSITION : Administrative, Exempt (12 months), Full-time (40 Hours) SALARY RANGE: $70,304-$70,304 HIRING RANGE: Anticipated hiring range is near or at the minimum of the classification, commensurate with candidate's education, experience, skills, and training. INTRODUCTORY PERIOD: Twelve (12) Months AVAILABLE: Immediately POSTING DATE: September 16 , 2026 APPLICATION DEADLINE: Open Until Filled, Apply Immediately. Definition of Classification: The General Accountant provides advanced professional accounting work involving the analysis, reconciliation, maintenance, and reporting of complex financial transactions and accounts. The position is responsible for documenting cash activities, preparation and review of journal entries, account analysis, and accounting principles and professional judgement. Responsibilities include oversight of bank account reconciliations, online payment system reconciliations, and other balance sheet account reconciliations to ensure the accuracy and integrity of the University’s financial records. Reporting to the Accounting Manager, this position exercises judgment and discretion in applying Generally Accepted Accounting Procedures (GAAP), University policies, and regulatory requirements.  This position is responsible for the accuracy and integrity of financial data, maintaining effective internal control, researching and resolving accounting issues, and supporting continuous improvement initiatives designed to strengthen financial stewardship and operational effectiveness across the University. Duties and Responsibilities: The duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to, the following: Prepare, analyze, and reconcile complex general ledger accounts. Research accounting discrepancies and determine appropriate accounting treatment Assist Accounting Manager with month-end, quarter-end, and year-end close activities. Analyze financial activity and recommend corrective actions. Administer and monitor University daily banking activity, including preparation of posting journals and consistent auditable records in accordance with GAAP. Perform numerous and complex reconciliations of cash, clearing, loan, student aid, and balance sheet accounts. Apply knowledge of GAAP and University policies and procedures in the preparation and review of adjusting, recurring, and special journal entries into the University’s general ledger. Review submitted journal entries and budget adjustments for accuracy, completeness, and compliance with University policies and procedures and communicate necessary corrections to originating departments. Review and reconcile donation transaction activities from University Advancement system and prepare appropriate financial transactions, calculate gains/losses, and prepare adjustments according to GAAP and University policies and procedures. Support the Accounting Manager in accounting operations, financial reporting, and strategic initiatives. Perform other related duties and special projects, as assigned. Qualifications Guidelines: Any combination of experience/training/education equivalent to, but not limited to, the following: Required Bachelor’s degree in Accounting, Business Administration, Finance, or a closely related field. Three years progressively responsible professional accounting experience. Knowledge of GAAP. Advanced Excel and ERP financial systems experience. Preferred Five years progressively responsible professional accounting experience. Experience in higher education or not for profit accounting. Knowledge and Skills: Knowledge of finance, accounting, budgeting, and cost control. Knowledge of applicable federal and state laws and regulations. University of Redlands Job Description: General Accountant Page 3 of 4 Work independently and exercise independent judgement with minimal supervision in a fast-paced environment with changing priorities. Prepare accurate reports, data entries, and files. • Communicate effectively and follow instructions, both verbally and in writing. Possess excellent technical, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Aptitude to learn new and evolving technology and recommend ways to improve position’s efficiency. Excellent technical, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Excellent organizational skills. Ability to operate standard office equipment such as 10-key calculator by touch. Advance proficiency in Microsoft Office software including Excel, Word, PowerPoint, and Outlook. Ability to recommend appropriate courses of action within established guidelines. Ability to foster strong, positive relationships and align both internal and external resources to achieve university objectives. Ability to exercise discretion and work with confidentiality. Must be able to competently interact with a culturally and ethnically diverse population of students, faculty, and staff. Physical Requirements/Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Working Conditions: Standard office setting. Duties performed are primarily in an office environment while sitting at a desk or computer workstation or in meetings. At least minimal environmental controls to assure health and comfort. Physical Demands: Incumbents regularly sit for long periods; walk short distances on a regular basis; may travel to various locations to attend meetings and conduct work; use hands and fingers to operate an electronic keyboard or other office machines; reach with hands and arms; stoop or kneel or crouch to file; climb stairs; speak clearly and distinctly to answer telephones and to provide information; see to read fine print and operate computer; hear and understand voices over telephone and in person; and lift, carry and/or move objects weighing up to 20 pounds. TO APPLY: A resume/CV and cover letter are required. In compliance with The Americans with Disabilities Act, if selected for the interview process and accommodations are needed, please call (909) 748-8040. If you are unable to complete an application due to a disability, contact us at (909) 748-8040 to ask for accommodation or an alternative application process. BACKGROUND CHECK: Employment is contingent upon successful completion of a criminal background check. Offers are conditional and may be rescinded if disqualifying information is found or if information was withheld or falsified. Current employees may be affected if the check is not satisfactorily completed. DRIVER’S LICENSE: Certain positions require a valid driver’s license and enrollment in the DMV Government Employer Pull Notice Program to verify license status and driving record. PHYSICAL/PSYCHOLOGICAL EXAMS: Select positions (e.g., Public Safety, Facilities, Student Affairs, Athletics, ITS) require a pre-employment physical exam, including the ability to lift 50+ lbs. Public Safety Officers must also pass a psychological evaluation. FOR MORE INFORMATION VISIT Human Resources (redlands.edu) SUBMISSION OF A RESUME OR APPLICATION INDICATES AGREEMENT THAT THE UNIVERSITY MAY VERIFY ANY AND ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED THEREIN. MEMBERS OF UNDERREPRESENTED GROUPS ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. For further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. Qualifications Skills Behaviors : Motivations : Education Experience Licenses & Certifications Equal Opportunity Employer/Protected Veterans/Individuals with Disabilities This employer is required to notify all applicants of their rights pursuant to federal employment laws. \nFor further information, please review the Know Your Rights notice from the Department of Labor. AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER You are using an unsupported browser. To use this site, please use a supported browser. Download Firefox Download Chrome Download Internet Explorer"
